ered a medium-strength starting hand\n- Has potential to make straights (with J-Q-A or 9-J-Q or 7-8-9-J sequences)\n- King high gives some showdown value\n\n**Position & Action:**\n- Opponent checked (called the big blind of 100 chips)\n- I'm in the big blind position, so I act last pre-flop\n- This is a relatively cheap spot to see the flop\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n\n1. **Check (0):** See the flop for free. K-10 offsuit has decent playability and can hit various boards. This minimizes risk while maintaining position advantage post-flop.\n\n2. **Raise (200+):** Build the pot with a hand that has decent equity. Could force opponent to fold weaker hands, but K-10 offsuit isn't strong enough to want to play a large pot out of position pre-flop.\n\n3. **Fold (-1):** Too conservative given we can check for free and see the flop.\n\n4. **All-in (-2):** Far too aggressive for K-10 offsuit. This is not a premium hand worth risking all chips on.\n\n**Game Context:**\n- Hand 12/20, so still mid-game\n- I'm ahead by 3,150 cumulative chips\n- Current stack: 19,900 chips (healthy)\n- Risk/reward doesn't favor aggressive pre-flop play here\n\nReason: \n\nK-10 offsuit is a playable hand but not strong enough to build a big pot pre-flop. Since my opponent just checked, I can see the flop for free and make a better decision based on what community cards come. Checking preserves chips, allows me to see if I connect with the board, and maintains my chip lead without unnecessary risk. Post-flop, I'll have better information to decide whether to continue.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
